  "summary": "Sir John Dodson (1780–1858) was an English civil and ecclesiastical lawyer, judge, and Tory MP. Educated at Merchant Taylors’ School and Oriel College, Oxford, he became an advocate at Doctors’ Commons in 1808 and edited reports of Admiralty Court cases.\n\nDodson represented Rye in Parliament from 1819 to 1823. He later served as Admiralty Advocate (1829–34) and King’s Advocate (1834–52), was knighted in 1834, and held several senior church-law offices. From 1852 he judged the Prerogative Court of Canterbury and served as Dean of the Arches until his death. He was also sworn to the Privy Council in 1852.\n\nHe married Frances-Priscilla Pearson in 1822. Their only son, John George Dodson, became an MP and was created Lord Monk Bretton. The article also traces Dodson’s extensive clerical family ancestry and lists his legal reports and writings, including his Admiralty Court reports and a judgment in the church-ornaments case *Liddell v. Westerton*.",
